course: dictionary
lesson: 6276
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them as separate numbers, and the sentence tells you which meaning to use. Monopoly is a good example: it can be a board game, or it can mean one company controls something all alone.
explain: «At Christmas Mia's cousin flips the Monopoly board across the room» is about the game, because you flip a board, not a company. The capital M and the lack of a before the word are clues that it is a name. When you are unsure, look at the words around it first.
explain: «You don't have a monopoly on the swing!» uses the words a monopoly on. This means having something all to yourself, so nobody else can share it. When a word is used in a new way, put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Monopoly with a capital M is the name of a board game where players buy streets with toy money.
fact: A monopoly means complete control of a product or service by one company or person, so nobody else sells it.
fact: «A monopoly on» means having something all to yourself.
fact: Because nobody else competes, a monopoly can keep prices high.
fact: Words that look the same can have different meanings, so the sentence decides which one you need.
exercise: «At Christmas the aunties ban Monopoly for good.» What is Monopoly here? | A kind of dice | A very long game of cards | A company that controls all the shops in town | *A board game where players buy streets with toy money
exercise: «You don't have a monopoly on the swing!» What does a monopoly on mean here? | Owning a shop that sells swings | Sharing a swing with everyone | Winning a game of tag | *Having something all to yourself
exercise: Why can a monopoly make prices high? | The company gives everything away for free | *Nobody else sells the product, so there is no competition | Customers choose the cheapest shop | The dice set the prices
test: «In Monopoly the brown streets are the cheapest and earn the least rent.» Which meaning of Monopoly is used here? | A company with no competition | *The board game | A kind of toy money | Having something all to yourself
test: «Mia says she has a monopoly on the best ideas.» What does she mean? | She is very tired | She owns a shop full of ideas | She is playing the board game | *She thinks she is the only one with good ideas
exam: «The banker handles the money in Monopoly, and a company with a monopoly can set high prices.» Which meaning of monopoly is used in the second part? | The board game | Having a swing all to yourself | A shop full of toy money | *Complete control of a product by one company
